  "body": "<p>After the Refuge’s costly defense, four companions turn the weather and ground against the cavern-bearing mountain. Their reasons differ, their storm does not become everything imagined, and escaping their own destruction becomes part of the work.</p><h2 id=\"four-reasons-to-go\">Four reasons to go</h2><p>Scribonia had prepared equations for combining storms. Eugene sat sharpening his sword, trying to make room for useful thoughts. Elenia wanted to bring the mountain down over the enemy beneath it and flood what remained. She also asked for Nymera’s ashes, promising to attempt her return after the offensive.</p><p>Elenia described a clean slate in which the land might heal. Scribonia offered a way to make the destruction happen. Eugene agreed to help while worrying about what such an attack might do to the people performing it. Arjahn was the bluntest: he wanted vengeance. For the moment, growth and repair were not what he came to seek.</p><p>They recognized that hidden people might be buried and that the scale could exceed the intended target. Asking for protection did not establish that those risks were removed. Their plan began inside that disagreement, not with a shared certainty that every consequence would be good.</p><h2 id=\"choosing-a-smaller-storm\">Choosing a smaller storm</h2><p>Scribonia favored using the ocean to feed a much larger weather system. Elenia wanted the work closer to the mountain and the caves they meant to strike. Arjahn moved between possibilities, then pressed them to choose rather than continue discussing a perfect plan.</p><p>The group took a known route toward the target. The jungle was strangely quiet. Eugene asked whether they were ready for the repercussions. Elenia wanted a chance to control the timing of a confrontation for once. Arjahn believed they could answer whatever retaliation followed. Eugene remained uneasy, but stayed beside them.</p><p>The roles settled into practice. Scribonia and Eugene would sustain separate storms. Elenia and Arjahn would redirect water and then break the mountain with earthquakes. A smaller, more focused attempt had replaced the most expansive proposal. Even that would become difficult to control from the ground beneath it.</p><h2 id=\"the-caster-left-with-a-monster\">The caster left with a monster</h2><p>Eugene had not finished learning the weather spell in the time available. He used Wish to supply what the plan required. Scribonia moved away to form the other system, while Elenia and Arjahn worked on the rivers. For a while their tasks seemed almost unexpectedly easy.</p><p>Then a beast came directly at Eugene, drawn toward the power he was invoking. Keeping his concentration now mattered as much as surviving its attack. He fought while maintaining his part of the storm, called for help, and finally sent the creature away. The others’ uninterrupted work did not mean everyone had been left undisturbed.</p><p>By the time the storms were ready, Eugene had already spent more than he hoped. Arjahn tried to reach him through the jungle, lost his bearings and rose above the trees to find his position. The offensive depended on people finding and protecting one another, not only on having enough power to break rock.</p><h2 id=\"what-the-storm-actually-became\">What the storm actually became</h2><p>Scribonia brought the warm and cold systems together. The resulting storm sent violent lightning over the mountain, with blizzard conditions above and pouring rain below. Updrafts threatened to tear trees free.</p><p>The result was powerful, but it did not acquire the greatest force Scribonia had imagined. He kept adjusting it toward rain. As the difference in temperature leveled out, wind diminished and water came down in heavy sheets. The actual weather, rather than the most ambitious theory discussed at the Refuge, became the weapon they had to work with.</p><p>Elenia prayed for anyone caught in its path. Arjahn asked his own Lord for a stronger blow, then returned to the task he could perform himself. Neither prayer supplied a witnessed account of hidden people reaching safety or a confirmed divine intervention in the storm.</p><h2 id=\"the-ground-opens\">The ground opens</h2><p>Elenia called an earthquake near the cavern approach. Arjahn did the same from the other side. Fissures split the ground and one of the caves broke open. Rain, loosened snow and shaking rock began an avalanche. Waterfalls appeared where there had been none before.</p><p>The danger was no longer only beneath the mountain. Water rose around Eugene and Arjahn. Eugene could not spare what he needed to fly. Arjahn lifted him into the air and carried him while still sustaining his own part of the destruction.</p><p>Elenia moved sideways out of the path of the falling mountain. A vast boulder passed close enough that agility would not have saved her from a direct hit. She kept looking for anyone else fleeing. Farther away, Scribonia maintained the storms; the ground around his companions was not the ground under his own feet.</p><h2 id=\"fire-water-and-a-hidden-result\">Fire, water and a hidden result</h2><p>A larger crack opened as the shaking ended. Red light showed through it, then lava met the flood. A towering mass of steam and smoke rose over the scene. Arjahn watched the flow, afraid they might send fire toward the Refuge. From his height he could see that the lava did not extend far beyond the base of the caves.</p><p>Elenia was knocked flat by a surge after releasing her spell. She used her means of moving across water to get higher and escape the falling debris. Arjahn carried Eugene toward somewhere safe to land and sent word that they were returning. Elenia answered that she was safe, if only just.</p><p>Behind them, rain, snowmelt and displaced boulders kept changing the mountainside. The smoke hid the final condition of the caves and anyone within them. The mountain was visibly reshaped. That was an outcome they could witness; a complete victory over the enemy and a count of everyone harmed were not.</p><h2 id=\"what-was-not-settled\">What was not settled</h2><p>The company had survived an offensive of its own making. It had not learned whether every captive had escaped or whether Abyss had been destroyed. Through the smoke, their immediate knowledge ended at the visible destruction.</p><p>Back at the base, Elenia took out Nymera’s ashes and called for her return. The words establish an attempt; a later living Nymera establishes that her history continued. Neither supplies the missing completion scene or a reliable name for the second restoration spell.</p><p>The later <a href=\"#babel-mist-expedition\">mist expedition</a> makes one consequence clearer: Abyss appears, mocks the attempt to bring a mountain down on him and then withdraws from the clearing. That later encounter establishes his continued activity. It does not give the assault’s participants that knowledge in advance, or explain every loss concealed by the storm.</p><p>Continue reading: <a href=\"#defense-of-skylight-refuge\">The Defense of Skylight Refuge</a> · <a href=\"#babel-mist-expedition\">The Western Mist · The Offer after the Clearing</a> · <a href=\"#return-to-babels-mist\">The Return to the Mist · Ryvyt’s Last Stand</a>.</p>",
